  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Serbia Innovates Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Serbia):  USAID&apos;s Serbia Innovates Activity focuses on solving low connectivity density and low collaboration within Serbia&apos;s innovation ecosystem; short innovation value chains; a lack of cooperation between innovative and traditional companies; lack of cooperation between innovative companies and financial institutions; and lack of financing options for these companies in the early stage of their development. The activity uses the &quot;supercluster model&quot; adjusted to the Serbian context. In the first six months of the activity, the implementer undertook extensive research on eight different domains within the economy: artificial intelligence, blockchain technology, high-technology agricultural production, gaming industry, solutions for smart environment, eco-smart energy sources, advanced materials and advanced production processes, and biotechnology. Through the research, they narrowed it down to the four most promising ones for focus: web3 and blockchain technologies; gaming and virtual reality; advanced solutions in medical technology and biotechnology, and high technology in agriculture and the food industry. The activity will build out the &quot;superclusters&quot;; i.e., the related networks to include the business producers, academia, government, and consumers, to accelerate economic potential of each domain. Superclusters apply principles of knowledge-based economic growth that are embraced in both the US and EU. As such, the activity will produce good platforms for cooperation with education institutions and start-up support services from the EU and US. This activity supports the USG&apos;s priority to grow economic opportunities and prosperity. It supports Embassy ICS Mission Objective 2.1 &quot;Broad-Based, Inclusive, and Sustainable Economic Growth...&quot; In line with USAID&apos;s pre-obligation requirement standards, the Mission verifies that all assistance activities do not have an adverse economic effect on the United States.</narrative>
  </description>
